</section>
<section id="S4.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.4 </span>Analysis of Self-attention with Modulation Term</h3>

<div id="S4.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We further examine MoSA from three distinct perspectives to assess its ability to capture temporal dependencies: visualization analysis, performance comparison with self-attention (SA) and complexity analysis.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:155.3pt;"><img src="2510.06680v1/Intra_TSA_matrix.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/176;" width="215" height="176" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.fig1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F3.fig1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Intra-patch correlations of MoSA.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F3.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_minipage ltx_align_center ltx_align_middle" style="width:155.3pt;"><img src="2510.06680v1/Inter_TSA_matrix.png" id="S4.F3.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:215/180;" width="215" height="180" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.fig2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F3.fig2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Inter-patch correlations of MoSA.</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Visualization analysis of temporal attention matrices.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS4.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.4.1 </span>Visualization Analysis</h4>

<div id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We conduct experiments on the ETTh1 dataset and visualize the intra- and inter-sequence temporal attention matrices generated from TimeFormer, with <math id="S4.SS4.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S=1</annotation></semantics></math>. Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.4 Analysis of Self-attention with Modulation Term ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ TimeFormer: Transformer with Attention Modulation Empowered by Temporal Characteristics for Time Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> illustrates the visualization results. Specifically, in the matrix of Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.4 Analysis of Self-attention with Modulation Term ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ TimeFormer: Transformer with Attention Modulation Empowered by Temporal Characteristics for Time Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the columns on the left represent earlier time steps of a subsequence, while in the matrix of Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 4.4 Analysis of Self-attention with Modulation Term ‣ 4 Experiment ‣ TimeFormer: Transformer with Attention Modulation Empowered by Temporal Characteristics for Time Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, the columns on the left represent earlier subsequences. First, we can observe that all non-zero elements in the two matrices are confined to the lower triangular part, which suggests that during the feature aggregation process (i.e., Eq.(<a href="#S3.E14" title="In 3.4.2 Unidirectional Past-to-future Influence ‣ 3.4 Self-Attention with Modulation Term (MoSA) ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ TimeFormer: Transformer with Attention Modulation Empowered by Temporal Characteristics for Time Series Forecasting" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>)), the features at the current time are solely influenced by previous time steps, thereby achieving temporal causality. Moreover, it can be observed from both matrices that the values of elements on the left are generally lower than those on the right. This indicates the decay effect that temporal influence decline over time. We can also observe that there are still several larger values at the leftmost time steps (see the red boxes), which suggests that while TimeFormer captures temporal dependencies, it also preserves the ability to extract global pairwise dependencies between tokens.</p>
</div>
</section>
